patchFile('siman_030/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt', 'pitchei-teshuva', {
  '1#_':
    'Treifah. Avodat HaGir — what he wrote; and in holes that do not have deficiency, etc.; and see in Dagul MeRevavah that he wrote that also the Tur\'s opinion is like Beit Yosef that he permits in this; only that for the law it seemed to him to be stringent as Shach\'s opinion — see there.',
  '2#_':
    'But as treifah. Avodat HaGir — regarding geese in which a hole is found in their skull bone from birth; and see in responsum of Shevut Ya\'akov, part 2, siman 65, that his opinion is like the lenient ones, and he testified thus in the name of his father-in-law the gaon Maharash, and all his household and grandsons the geonim ruled to permit; and not as written in responsum of Chinuch, Beit Yosef, that one should be careful ab initio not to buy such geese, for it is an extra stringency — see there; and see in responsum of Noda B\'Yehuda, part Yoreh De\'ah, siman 5, that he also raised to permit if the hole is small that there is no doubt that it would not reach by itself the measure of sela in behemah; but if it is large that there is doubt regarding it from the aspect of deficiency of the skull — one should not permit except b\'dieved — see there; and see in responsum of Pri Tevuah, siman 71, that he wrote received from his rabbis that a thin hole into which only a human hair enters — there is no doubt on it that certainly it does not have according to proportion the measure of sela in behemah; and in this I practice to permit simply even in a safek whether it waited twelve months. And regarding twelve months I practice to permit even without examination — see there; and see in Noda B\'Yehuda, second edition, part 4, siman 12 and 18 and 19; and in responsum of Shetei HaLechem, siman 42, more on this. [And see in responsum of Chasam Sofer, siman 55, that he raised: these land birds such as chickens that have a hole from the beginning of their creation and a recognizable bump on their head that they call "tufts" — even if we say by itself their smallness has in the hole the measure of deficiency of the skull, nevertheless they are permitted since it is not removed from the living but lacking from the beginning of its creation; only Peri Chadash wrote that one should be stringent for himself, and so we practice; but from the essence of the law it appears to permit; and indeed water birds such as geese whose membrane is soft and the bone protects the membrane — if the bone is not whole, the membrane will eventually be pierced — treifah, even from the beginning of its creation, and even if it lives many days and lays eggs, for although now it produces eggs, since the membrane is not yet pierced, nevertheless since the membrane will eventually be pierced even after many years, it is already rendered treifah today; and so in Shulchan Aruch HaRav; and so they practice here and in the community of Amsterdam and Frankfurt and in all communities of Israel — and not like Noda B\'Yehuda on this — see there.]',
});

// --- rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd (2) ---
patchFile('siman_030/rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd/part-001.txt', 'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd', {
  '1#_':
    '(Siman 30, Shach, se\'if kaf dalet) But because the membrane will eventually tear. It is wondrous to me — for on the contrary it is explained in Tosafot there that in a dent it is not counted, since it is only because the membrane will eventually tear; if so, from their objection to consider a skull that was crushed in its majority it is proven that this treifah is on its own account; if so, if we come to declare treifah holes in the skull that do not have deficiency — it must be by the law of nichbesah; if so, it is on its own account; and this requires great examination (afterward I found in Ezrat Nashim that the later ones stood on this).',
  '2#_':
    '(Seif 2, Hagahah) That we are not concerned in behemah for piercing of the brain membrane. And nevertheless examination is required with the eye\'s appearance that the membrane was not pierced. Turei Zahav.',
});

// --- nekudot-hakesef (1) ---
patchFile('siman_030/nekudot-hakesef/part-001.txt', 'nekudot-hakesef', {
  '1#_':
    '(Siman 30, Shach, se\'if heh) I wrote that examination is required, for I was drawn after the four Derishah; and now I examined that the words of Beit Yosef and Shulchan Aruch are correct, for thus said the Rosh: the cord that encircles the pot — meaning that which encircles the pot from inside, and it is literally like a sela, only that a measure more than sela is required, for if it were literally a sela it would not be enough that a sela enter into it — literally in literal [measure]; and if so, when there is a pot more than sela — that is, that the cord encircling the pot inside is the length of a tefach — if so, it is literally like a sela, for when you place that cord on the sela around on its lip precisely, it will encircle the sela; it is found that the width of the sela itself is one-third tefach; and this is clear — not like the Derishah; and examine this.',
});

// --- kaf-hachayim (2) ---
patchFile('siman_030/kaf-hachayim/part-001.txt', 'kaf-hachayim', {
  '1#_':
    '(a) [Seif 1] Behemah or chayah where most of the skull bone was crushed. In the Gemara it is stated (Chullin 42b): a skull that was crushed in its majority — treifah; and Rashi explained that it was struck with many blows and did not lose substance and did not pierce the membrane; and so Ri"v — the skull was compressed and shaken by the blows, and it is close to breaking but not actual breaking; and so Shach, se\'if alef, and Peri Chadash, section 2, like Rashi. However, Perishah and Derishah expanded on this matter, and from their words it appears that the skull was cracked is called nichbesah, even though the entire skull is whole — see there; and Beit Din, Turei Zahav, section 1, brought further support for Perishah\'s words from the words of the poskim; therefore he wrote to be stringent, and even if it was not compressed, only cracked alone — whether in most of its height or in most of the circumference — treifah; see there. And so Kereti, section 2, that one should be stringent like all the explanations. And so Shafan Dam, section 1; Chayyei Adam, general rule 15, section 5; Einei HaKohen, siman 2, section 1; Kometz HaMinchah, section 1; Beit Yitzchak, section 1; Zivchei Tzedek, section 1.',
  '2#_':
    '(b) And likewise — if it was not cracked, only compressed like a long strip in a small width — its law is like cracked. Turei Zahav there. Einei HaKohen there. Beit Yitzchak in Tuv Ta\'am, section 2; Mikdash Me\'at, section 5.',
});

// --- yad-avraham (2) ---
patchFile('siman_030/yad-avraham/part-001.txt', 'yad-avraham', {
  '1#_':
    '(Siman 30, seif 1) Behemah or chayah whose skull bone was crushed, etc. — treifah. In Hagahot Ashiri, Elu Treifot, in the name of Ravyah he wrote: and likewise in a bird (and so is in Yerushalmi, end of chapter 3 of Niddah) — nichbesah means that it was struck with many blows and did not lose substance; and even pierced in any amount that goes through — there is none; and therefore kosher, even in waterfowl. And it appears to me that even if pierced thereby it is kosher if there is no measure to declare treifah thereby in a land bird, unless the illness entirely dominated the membrane, as written siman 33, seif 4, in Hagahah; and see in this siman. And who knows — perhaps nichbesah means it was cracked.',
  '2#_':
    '(There) Between most of its height and most of the circumference — treifah. Explained in Rambam, chapter 10 of Hilchot Shechitah, that it is only a safek treifah, for it arose in his analysis in the Gemara whether the treifot depend on its height or on its circumference; even though most of the circumference is greater than most of the height, as Rashi and Ran and Shitah Mekubetzet wrote — nevertheless it is possible that the main vitality depends on the height. And likewise regarding the brain there is an opinion that treifah depends on the lower and not the upper, as the Taz wrote in the beginning of siman 31, even though the upper is thick and stronger; and as Tosafot wrote (daf 46, beginning of 2a) and the Rosh, Elu Treifot, siman 11. And there is a difference regarding whether there is another safek — there is a safek-safek; and as written below, seif 4, regarding joining the majority that one may be lenient — se\'if kaf vav.',
});

// --- yad-ephraim (1) ---
patchFile('siman_030/yad-ephraim/part-001.txt', 'yad-ephraim', {
  '1#_':
    'That it is one-third tefach — treifah. Avodat HaGir; and see in Mishna Berurah that he also brought many who permit; and see in responsum of Shevut Ya\'akov, part 2, siman 25, on chickens and geese that I raised in my book Eretz Yisrael and in Sulam Lamincah according to the lenient ones; and so printed in Likutei Halachot there; and so I saw in the house of Moharach and his sons and grandsons the geonim to permit. But I saw in responsum of Chavot Yair from his own reasoning that one should be careful ab initio not to buy such geese; and accordingly some teachers practice to rule thus. And it is a stringency without reason, for if so, also among doves we find thus, and we say in the Gemara: a dove they never forbade us; and likewise in Orach Chayyim, general rule 56, it is written; and so turtledoves and doves — we do not find in their species a prohibition; and if it were according to the stringent ones, it would be found in their species of prohibition, and we have no authority to add another sign of impurity that we do not find in the signs of impure birds, end of his words; and his proof is wondrous — what relevance is a sign of impurity here? We come from the aspect of deficiency of the skull. And what is found "in its species of prohibition" — that is, he only said regarding those that do not contain one of the eighteen treifot; and these, since there is deficiency in their skulls, are treifot by circumstance, and through corruption of formation treifot came about in them from birth, like if there is deficiency in another limb from birth that was rendered treifah therein; and see what I wrote above on the words of Noda B\'Yehuda on this. And he wrote in Noda B\'Yehuda, siman 5: regarding geese in which a hole is found in their skull bones and it is recognizable that it is from birth — (1) if the hole is small that there is no doubt that it would not reach by itself the measure of sela in behemah, I permit; but if it is already large that there is doubt regarding it from deficiency of the skull, I send it to another teacher who permits it, and when there is no other teacher who volunteers to permit it — then b\'dieved I also permit it, since from the beginning of its creation one may say treifah of piercing the skull bone means piercing by force. Also one may join to this that one may say the skin seals the hole — even though for Shach, siman 46, in a gullet there is a measure for its deficiency, it does not seal; nevertheless it appears fit to join in such a case — see there; and see what I wrote in my book Beit Ephraim, siman 23, to answer what is written in the book Chiddushei HaRazah on the words of Or Zarua, Turei Zahav, that he raised to forbid; and in Knesset HaGedolah, siman 48; and there in siman 39, siman 426: And Mishna Berurah wrote in the name of Shulchan Gavo\'ah: even if there is some lesion on its head, many slaughterers declare treifah even though the skull bone is whole — they err; rather one removes the scab or shell that is on the head where the lesion is; and if the bone beneath it is whole — kosher; and if not — treifah from safek whether the brain membrane was pierced. And if before checking whether the bone is whole or not the head was lost — kosher because of safek-safek.',
});

// --- mateh-yehonatan (2) ---
patchFile('siman_030/mateh-yehonatan/part-001.txt', 'mateh-yehonatan', {
  '1#_':
    '(Siman 30, seif 1) Between most of the height and most of the circumference. Explained: the majority of the quantity of the height or of the circumference; but if one strip was crushed, even though it rose over the majority of its height or circumference — kosher.',
  '2#_':
    '(There, seif 2) It lost substance and lacks from it — so too as a sela — treifah. This is the language of the Tur; and if it lost substance and lacks from it kesela — Rashi wrote that it is treifah; and Baal HaItur wrote that it is kosher; and so it appears from the words of Or Zarua, the Rosh, and Beit Yosef — the words of our rabbi do not appear correct in this, for behold the Rosh wrote: that which Rabbenu Chananel said kesela is like more than kesela — and presumably he adopts Rashi\'s explanation; and my teacher, Rabbeinu Yonah, said: this difficulty may be dismissed and one may say the Rosh brings that of Rabbenu Chananel regarding impurity, not regarding treifah at all; but if it is difficult — behold this is the language of the Rosh in a bird: what? — Rashi\'s explanation: holes of deficiency of a bird that is not in my gullet like issur — how much do we estimate, etc.; and from the words of Rav Alfasi it appears, etc.; and it is difficult for Rashi\'s explanation, etc.; and furthermore, what did he need this measure — if we estimate in a bird more than other prohibitions, such as deficiency of skull like a drill, and according to Beit Hillel kesela, end of his words. Behold it explicitly appears from his words that deficiency of the skull is treifah; and it must be that certainly the Rosh holds like Baal HaItur; and that which he wrote thus — meaning: what did he strain Rashi to explain thus, and not like the words of Rav Alfasi — because it was difficult for him: what did he need, etc.; and Rashi, according to his view, holds deficiency of the skull is treifah at kesela.',
});

